entry.sense:
to sing, Il., etc.:—then of any sound,
to twang, of the bowstring, Od.;
to whistle, of the wind, Mosch.;
to ring, of a stone struck, Theocr.

that may be taken or
conquered, Hdt.;
that may be understood, Plat.

of or
for fishing, Xen., Arist.; —ἡ ἁλιευτική (with or without τέχνη)
the art of fishing, Plat.

a glade or
grove, Lat. saltus, Hom.;
grove, Od., Hdt., etc.

from grief,
to be ill at ease, be distraught, Il.:
to be beside oneself, Hom., Soph.

on or
at both sides, Il.;
with both hands at once, id=Il.

Pass.
to be undone, κατὰ τί δεῦρʼ ἀνεφθάρης;
by what ill luck came you hither? Ar.

a rival in love, τινός
for another, Ar.:
a rival, Plat.

Mid.,
to exchange one thing
for another:

comp. ἀνωτέρω, absol.
higher, Aesch.;
further, Hdt.

adv. of place
from above, from on high, Hdt., Trag., etc.; ὕδατος ἄνωθεν γενομένου, i. e. rain, Thuc.:
from the upper country, from inland, id=Thuc.

Apeiraean, Od.;
Apeire =
limitless-land, an imaginary place.

to give up in despair, to despair, NTest.; (others
to hope to receive from another);
to drive to despair, τινά Anth.

to guide aright, to direct, govern, id=Soph.;
to correct, chastise, Eur.: c. inf.
to direct one to do a thing, Aesch.

Adv. of ἄπνευστος, ἀπ. ἔχειν
to hold one's breath, Plat.;
without drawing breath, Dem.

devoted to enjoyment, Arist.;
producing enjoyment, id=Arist.:—adv., ἀπολαυστικῶς ζῆν to live a life
of pleasure, id=Arist.

when it stands alone it usually expects a negative answer, like Lat. num? attic; so ἆρα μή;
num vero? Aesch.:—for an affirmative answer, ἆρʼ οὐ; ἆρʼ οὐχί;
nonne vero? is used, Soph., etc.

of or
for reckoning, arithmetical, Plat.: ἡ ἀριθμητική (sc. τέχνη)
arithmetic, id=Plat.

of oracles,
to utter, proclaim, tell of, id=Soph.;
to speak out concerning a thing, Aesch.

of or
for the flute, Plat.; ἡ -κή (sc. τέχνη)
flute-playing, id=Plat.

to lie in the court-yard, of cattle, Od.;
to pass the night, lodge, Eur.; of soldiers,
to bivouac, Hdt.

of or
for an autocrat: adv. -κῶς,
despotically, Plut.

of things,
to get rid of, δίψαν Il.;
to shed its blossoms, of plants, Od.;
to slacken its force, of a dart, Il.

a mist, Lat. caligo, Od.;
a mist over the eyes of one dying, Il.; or of a person deprived of the power of knowing others, id=Il.—metaph.
gloom, trouble, Aesch.

of solids,
the chaff that flies off in winnowing, in pl., Il.;
the down on fruits, Anth.

of or
for cattle-driving:— ἡ βοηλατική (sc. τέχνη)
the herdsman's art, Plat.

Lat. fremitus, any loud noise, as the
crackling of fire, Il.;
roaring of a storm, Aesch.;
neighing of horses, id=Aesch.

EPEXEGETIC, where γάρ is used to begin a promised narration, λεκτέα ἃ γιγνώσκω· ἔχει γὰρ ἡ χώρα πεδία κάλλιστα I must relate what I know;
now, the country has most beautiful plains, Xen.; so, after the introductory forms, σκέψασθε δέ, δῆλον δέ, τεκμήριον δέ, μαρτύριον δέ, μέγιστον δέ, Plat., etc.; τούτου δὲ τεκμήριον· τόδε γάρ . . , Hdt.:—in ἀλλὰ γάρ, a clause must be supplied between ἀλλά and γάρ, as, ἀλλὰ γὰρ ἤκουσα
but [say no more], for I heard, Aesch.

a question, like Lat. nam, Engl.
why, what, τίς γάρ σε ἧκεν;
why who hath sent thee? Il.; τί γάρ;
quid enim? i. e. it must be so, Soph.

a milk-pail, Od.:
a water-bucket, Hdt.;
any round vessel, a bee-hive, Anth.;
a drinking-bowl, Theocr.

metaph.
to taste, feel, δουρὸς ἀκωκῆς, ὀϊστοῦ γεύσασθαι Hom.; γευσόμεθʼ ἀλλήλων ἐγχείαις
let us try one another with the spear, Il.:
to taste the sweets of, ἀρχῆς, ἐλευθερίης Hdt.;
to have experience of, μόχθων, πένθους Soph., Eur.

of or
for geometry, geometrical, Plat.: γεωμετρικὴ (sc. τέχνη),
geometry, id=Plat.

mostly in pl. Γίγαντες,
the Giants, a savage race destroyed by the gods, Od.;
the sons of Gaia, Hes.

of or
for cookery: adv. -κῶς,
like a cook, artistically, Ar.

Dep.: of persons,
to hurt, do a mischief to, Hom.; μή με δηλήσεται (epic for -ηται) Od.; so in Hdt.;
to hurt by magic potions, Theocr.

as interrog., τί δή ποτε;
what in the world? quid tandem? id=Dem.; πόσοι δή ποτε; how many
do you suppose? id=Dem.

perhaps, it may be, Il.; in attic
doubtless, I suppose, I presume, of course, Lat. scilicet, οὐ δήπου τλητόν Aesch., etc.: often in phrases, ἴστε γὰρ δή που, μέμνησθε γὰρ δή που Dem.; so, as interrog. implying an affirm. answer, τὴν αἰχμάλωτον κάτοισθα δή που;
I presume you know, Soph.

hard to use, nearly useless, Xen.;
intractable, id=Xen.:—adv. -τως ἔχειν
to be in distress, Plut.

strengthd. for εἰ,
if really, if indeed, Hom., etc.; also,
even if, even though, id=Hom.

if anywhere, if at all, Lat. si-cubi, Hom., etc.; εἴ τί που ἐστίν,
if it is
any way possible, Od.

of or
for peace, peaceful, Plat., etc.: adv. -κῶς,
peaceably, Xen.

to land one
from a ship,
disembark, id=Thuc.

OF TIME, ἐξ οὗ or ἐξ οὗτε [χρόνου], Lat. ex quo, since, Hom., attic; ἐκ τοῦ or ἐκ τοῖο
from that time, Il.; ἐκ πολλοῦ (sc. χρόνου)
for a long time, Thuc.

to draw, drag, Lat. traho, with a notion of force, ποδὸς ἕλκε
began to drag [the dead body] by the foot, Il.;
to draw ships down to the sea, Od.; of mules,
to draw a chariot, id=Od.;
to draw the plough
through the field, id=Od.

τὸ Ἑλληνικόν
the Greeks collectively, Hdt.;
the Greek soldiery, Xen.

c. acc.
to appease, Hom.;
to make up for, Xen.

entry.etym:
from εἴκω, of which we have 3rd sg. imperf. εἶκε,
it seemed good, Il.; fut. εἴξω,
will be like, Ar.

entry.sense:
to pour wine
over a thing, Il.;
to pour a libation, Od.

ἐπὶ πλέον
still more, Hdt.;
before, Lat. coram, ἦγον αὐτὸν ἐπὶ τὰ κοινά id=Hdt.

of Place, denoting Rest
upon, as in ἐπίκειμαι, or Motion,
upon or
over, as in ἐπιβαίνω;
to or
towards, as in ἐπαρίστερος, ἐπιδέξιος;
against, as in ἐπαΐσσω, ἐπιστρατεύω;
up to a point, as in ἐπιτελέω;
over or
beyond boundaries, as in ἐπινέμομαι, ἐπεργασία.

Pass.
to grow up after, as posterity, Lat. succrescere, id=Hdt.;
to grow up as a successor, id=Hdt.

of men,
conspicuous, famous, distinguished by rank, Hdt.;
notable, for well or ill, Thuc., Xen.

of or
for a statuary: ἡ -κη (sc. τέχνη),
the art of statuary, Luc.

the furthest part, edge, border, verge, Hom., Hdt., attic: in pl.
the borders, Hdt.;
the extremities of the world, id=Hdt.

on, for or
during the day, the day through, Od.;
by day, Pind.

to oversee, observe, survey, of the sun, Hom.:—then of the gods,
to watch over, observe, take notice of, Od., Hdt., etc.; of a general going his rounds, Thuc.;
to visit the sick, Xen.

of or
for driving, Plat.: ἡ -κή (sc. τέχνη)
the art of driving, id=Plat.

from morn, i. e.
at dawn, at break of day, Hom., etc.;
this morning, Od.

of or
for a surgeon: — ἡ -κή (sc. τέχνη),
surgery, medicine, Hdt., Plat., etc.

peculiar, separate, distinct, ἔθνος ἴδιον Hdt.; ἴδιοί τινες θεοί Ar.; ἴδιον ἢ ἄλλοι
peculiar and different from others, Plat.;
strange, unaccustomed, ἰδίοισιν ὑμεναίοισι Eur.

any loud sound: the shout or
cry of men, Il.;
the sound of the lyre, Od.; of the wind, of a fire, Il.

for καθʼ ἅ,
according as, just as, Xen.:— so καθάπερ, ionic κατάπερ, Hdt., Ar., etc.:—strengthd., καθάπερ εἰ, ionic κατάπερ εἰ,
like as if, exactly as, Hdt., Plat.; καθάπερ ἄν Dem.; καθάπερ ἂν εἰ Plat., etc.

of or
for beautiful dances, Eur., Ar.:— ὁ κ. a spring near Eleusis,
the fount of goodly dances, Hhymn., Eur.

a dry stalk, Lat. palea, stipula, a chip of wood, Ar.: in pl.
the dry sticks of cinnamon, Hdt.;
dry twigs, chips, straws, bits of wool, such as birds make nests of, Ar.

preparation, ἐν κατασκευῇ τοῦ πολέμου
in preparing for it, Thuc.;
the equipment of ships, engines, etc., id=Thuc.

a sudden turn or
end, a close, conclusion, Aesch.; of death, Soph., Thuc.: in drama,
the catastrophe, Luc.

to lie sick or
wounded, lie in misery, Hom., Soph., etc.;
to lie at the mercy of the conqueror, Aesch.

any sharp sound, such as
the twang of a bow, Il.;
the scream of cranes, Hom.;
the grunting of swine, Od.;
the hissing of serpents, Aesch.;
the barking of dogs, Xen.:—also of song, Soph.; κλ. δύσφατος, of Cassandra-prophecies, Aesch.

of or
for embellishment:— ἡ -κή (sc. τέχνη),
the art of embellishment, Plat.

more dog-like, i. e.
more shameless, more audacious, Hom.;
more horrible, κύντερον ἄλλο ποτʼ ἔτλης Od.

to receive as produce or
profit, Ar., Plat.;
to purchase Ar.

to rub smooth, pound in a mortar, Lat. levigare, Hdt.;
to grind with the teeth, Xen.:— generally,
to crush, extirpate, Hdt.

a scale, husk, λ. ᾠοῦ an egg-
shell, Ar.;
the cup of a filbert, Anth.

adv.
shrilly, Hom.;
clearly, Il.:—neut. as adv., λιγὺ μέλπεσθαι Hes., Aesch.

to abuse, revile, Hdt., attic;
to rebuke, Xen.:—Mid.
to rail at one another, Ar.

to visit, νέον μεθέπεις;
dost thou come but now
to visit us? Od.

of or
for measuring, Plat.: ἡ -κή (sc. τέχνη)
mensuration, id=Plat.

of or
for metre, metrical, Arist.: τὰ -κά and ἡ -κή (sc. τέχνη),
prosody, id=Arist.

after interrogatives, it mostly takes somewhat of an objective force, τί μήν;
quid vero? what
then? i. e.
of course, naturally so, Aesch., etc.; τί μὴν οὐ;
well, why not? Eur.; πῶς μήν;
well, but how . . ? Xen.

with Ind., implying a negat. answer,
surely not, you don't mean to say that, Lat. num? whereas with οὐ an affirm. answer is expected, Lat. nonne? ἆρʼ οὐ τέθνηκε;
surely he is dead, is he not? ἆρα μὴ τέθνηκε;
surely he is not dead, is he?:—when οὐ and μή appear in consecutive clauses, each negat. retains its proper force, οὐ σῖγʼ ἀνέξει μηδὲ δειλίαν ἀρεῖς;
will you not be silent, and
will you be cowardly? i. e. be silent and be not cowardly, Soph.

of or
for letting out:— ἡ μισθωτική, = μισθαρνική,
a mercenary trade, Plat.

= μνημεῖον
a monument, Plat.;
an epitaph, Arist.

the belly, paunch, Il., Hdt.;
the womb, Il.: metaph., of
earth, gremium telluris, Eur.

of or
for a ship, δόρυ νήιον or νήιον alone,
ship-timber, Hom.

metaph.
like a child, childish, silly, Hom., Hes.;
without forethought, Hom., Aesch.

of or
for a herdsman's life, nomadic, pastoral, Arist.:—adv. -κῶς,
like Nomads, Strab.

a way, path, track, road, highway: ποταμοῦ ὁδός
the course of a river, Xen.;
the path of the heavenly bodies, Eur.

of or
from the whole world (ἡ οἰκουμένη); of Eccl. Councils,
oecumenical.

of or
for housekeeping: hence οἰκούρια (sc. δῶρα) τά,
wages, reward for keeping the house, Soph.

to go, come, Od.;
to walk, i. e.
to live, Soph.

of or
for an omen: ἡ -κή (sc.τέχνη),
augury, Plat.

ὅπως is used as the echo to a preceding πῶς; in dialogue:
A. καὶ πῶς;
B. ὅπως; [d'ye ask]
how? Ar.;
A. πῶς με χρὴ καλεῖν;
B. ὅπως; id=Ar.

of or
for the desires, appetitive, Arist.; τὸ ὀρεκτικόν,
the appetites, id=Arist.

to set in motion, θυμὸν ὁρμ.
to gasp out one's life, Aesch.;
to excite, urge, Pind.

of or
for bird-catching: —ἡ -κή (sc. τέχνη),
the art of bird-catching, fowling, Plat.

anything made of such trees,
a goad, Hes.;
a lance, Eur.

still more generally,
a sound, of the harp, Hhymn.;
the din of battle, Hes.

for ὅτʼ ἄν (ὅτε ἄν), adverb of Time,
whenever, Lat. quandocunque, foll. by Subjunct., Hom., etc.; in epic also ὅτε κεν Il.:— εἰς ὅτε κεν
until such time as. . , Od.:— ὅταν τάχιστα, Lat. quum primum, Xen.

= ὅ τι,
wherefore, ὁτιὴ τί;
why so? wherefore so? id=Ar.

any loud noise, as
the din of battle, Hes.;
the rattling of chariots, Aesch.;
the crash of thunder, Soph.; also of the flute, γλυκὺν αὐλῶν ὄτ. id=Soph.

elliptic, in interrogative replies, where
yes must be supplied, τούτους ἀγαθοὺς ἐνόμισας; Answ. οὐ γάρ . . ;
yes, for why shouldn't I? yes; why not? Ar.

of or
for teaching: —ἡ -κή (sc. τέχνη),
education, Plat.; so, τὸ παιδευτικόν Plut.

absol.
to stand one's ground, stand fast, Il., Hdt., attic;
to remain with the army, Thuc.

a clatter, crash, of trees falling, Il.;
a chattering of teeth, id=Il.;
the plash of a body falling into water, id=Il.; the
rattling or
crash of thunder, Ar.:
a clashing of arms, Hdt., Trag.

for Πέλοπος νῆσος,
the Peloponnesus, now
the Morea, Hhymn., etc.

as Subst.
that which surrounds, as
the rim of a shield, Eur.;
the string that runs round the top of a net (cf. ἐπίδρομος), Xen.;
a gallery running round a building, id=Xen.

Causal, to denote the object
about or
for which one does something:

of Manner,
in what way? how? Od., etc.; πῆ δή;
how tell me? id=Od.; πῆ μάλιστα;
how exactly? Plat.:—also in indirect questions, ἐκαραδόκεον τὸν πόλεμον κῆ ἀποβήσεται Hdt., etc.

πῆ μέν . . , πῆ δέ . . ,
on one side . . , on the other . . , Plut.;
partly . . , partly . . , Xen.

Mid.
to make manifest, Il.;
to tell of, disclose, Hom., Hes.

c. gen. rei,
a larger share of a thing, Arist.;
gain made from a thing, Dem.

of or
for washing: Πλυντήρια (sc. ἱερά), τά, a festival at Athens,
in which the clothes of Athena's statue were washed, Xen., etc.

of the cause,
whence? wherefore? id=Aesch.; alone, πόθεν;
how can it be? impossible! Eur., Ar.

interrog. adv.
where? Od., Soph.;—c. gen., πόθι Νύσας;
in what part of Nysa? Eur.

ἐκ πολλοῦ
from a great distance, Thuc.;
for a long time, v. ἐκ II. 1.

of Value,
how much? Ar.; πόσου;
for how much? at what price? Lat. quanti? id=Ar.; so, ἐπὶ πόσῳ; Plat.

which of a number? Lat. quotus? πόστον δὴ ἔτος ἐστὶν ὅτε ξείνισσας ἐκεῖνον;
how many years is it since . . ? Od.:—in indirect questions, πόστῳ μέρει
with how small a part, Xen.

interrog. adv., in direct or indirect questions, corresponding to the relat. ὅπου,
where? Lat. ubi? Hom., etc.:—c. gen. loci, ποῦ γῆς; ποῦ χθονός;
where in theworld? Lat. ubinam terrarum? Aesch., etc.; so, ποῦ ποτʼ εἶ φρενῶν; Soph.; ποῦ γνώμης εἶ; Id=Soph..; ποῦ τύχης;
at what point of fortune? Id=Soph.

the treatment of a subject, id=Plat.;
a treatise, Arist.;
an historical work, systematic history, Polyb., Luc.

to take the first place, be best, Soph.:—c. gen.
to rank before, take precedence of others, πρ. τῶν πολλῶν Plat.;
to rule over, Ὀλύμπου πρ. Soph.

an approach, of
the foot of a mountain, Anth.;
the mouth of a river, id=Anth.

a servant, Soph., Eur.;
a ministering priest, Trag.; πρ. φόνου
minister of death, Aesch.

of or
from a ship's stern, κάλως Eur.:—neut. pl. πρυμνήσια (sc. δεσμά)
stern-cables, Lat. retinacula navis, Hom.

πῶ μάλα; or πώμαλα;
where in the world? how in the name of fortune? i. e.
not a whit, Ar., Dem.

interrog. adv. of manner,
how? in what way or
manner? Lat. qui? quomodo? used in direct questions, as ὅπως in indirect, Hom., etc.:—with a second interrog. in the same clause, πῶς ἐκ τίνος νεὼς . . ἥκετε;
how and by what ship came ye? Eur.:—c. gen., πῶς ἀγῶνος ἥκομεν;
how are we come off in it? id=Eur.

πῶς οὐ . . ;
how not so . . ; ? i. e.
surely it is so . . , Thuc., etc.

of or
for a rhapsodist: ἡ -κή (with and without τέχνη),
the rhapsodist's art, Plat.

generally,
a sign to do or
begin something, Od.;
a watchword, Eur.;
a battle-sign, signal, id=Eur.

conveying goods:— τὰ σκ.
baggage-wagons, Plut.;
transport vessels, Strab.

in less good sense,
a sly trick, artifice, Eur., Thuc.;
a stage-trick, claptrap, Ar.

a swathing band, and in pl.
swaddling-clothes, Hhymn., Pind.; παῖς ἔτʼ ὢν ἐν σπαργάνοις Aesch.;
tokens by which a person is identified, Lat. monumenta, crepundia, Soph., Ar.

of persons,
earnest, serious, Xen.;
active, zealous, Plut.

of weapons,
the point, Il.;
the edge of a sword, NTest.:—also like Lat. acies, the front, στόμα πολέμοιο, ὑσμίνης Il.; so alone, Xen.

to turn to or
from an object,
to turn back, return, id=Il., Soph.; στραφέντες ἔφευγον Xen.

a permit or
licence to reside, given to aliens, Ar.;
a ticket given by each person who joined in a picnic, to be presented for payment at the end (cf. συμβολή IV), id=Ar.

intr.
to fall upon also, to join in attacking, Xen.;
to make a joint inroad, Dem.

to assist in raising: Pass., aor1 part. συνεξαρθείς
being lifted up at once, Plut.;
being excited at the same time, Luc.

of or
for slinging.:— ἡ -κή (sc. τέχνη),
the art of slinging, Plat.

of or
for stewardship:—at Rome,
belonging to the quaestorship, Plut.

of Time, ἡ τελευταία, with or without ἡμέρα, the
last day allowed for payment, Dem.;
one's last day, Soph.

in sg.
a vessel of any kind,
a bathing-tub, Aesch.;
a cinerary urn, id=Aesch., Soph.;
a balloting-urn, Aesch.;
a vase for libations, id=Aesch.;
a vase or
ewer for water, Eur.;
a pot or
jar, Xen.; ξύλινα τ.
chests, id=Xen.

an assessment of damages, Aeschin., etc.;
a rating or
assessment, Arist.

τίποτε, or τί ποτε;
what or
why, tell me? Lat. quid tandem? Soph.

in direct questions,
who? which? neut.
what? which? Lat. quis, quae, quid?, Hom., etc.: relating to other words in the same case, τίς δʼ οὗτος ἔρχεαι;
who art thou
that comest? Il.; τίνʼ ὄψιν σὴν προσδέρκομαι;
what face is this I see of thine? Eur.:— τίς ἄν or κεν, with the opt., expresses doubt,
who could, who would do so? Hom.: —in double questions, τί λαβόντα τί δεῖ ποιεῖν;
what has one received and
what must one do? Dem.; so, τίς πόθεν εἶς ἀνδρῶν;
who and whence art thou? Od.

τίς with Particles: τίς γάρ; Lat. quisnam? why who? who possibly? Il., etc.; τίς δή;
who then? Theogn.; τίς δῆτα; Soph.; τίς ποτε;
who in the world? who ever? Xen.

neut. τί; as a simple question,
what? Aesch.; also,
why? Il. b. τί μοι; τί σοι;
what is it to me? to thee? Soph., etc.; c. gen., τί μοι ἔριδος;
what have I to do with the quarrel? Il.; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σοί;
what is there (in common) to me and thee?
what have I to do with thee? NTest. c. τί with Particles:— τί γάρ;
why not? how else? Lat. quid enim? quidni? i. e.
of course, no doubt, Aesch., etc.; τί δέ; Lat. quid vero? Plat.; τί δέ, εἰ . .; but
what, if . .? Eur.; τί δή; τί δήποτε;
why ever?
why in the world? Plat.:— τί μή;
why not? Lat. quidni? Trag.:— τί μή; i. e.
yes certainly, Plat., etc.

τίς; τί; with part., followed by a verbal clause, forms one sentence in Greek where we use two, εἴρετο τίνες ἐόντες προαγορεύουσι;
who they were that proclaim? Hdt.; καταμεμάθηκας τοὺς τί ποιοῦντας τοὔνομα τοῦτʼ ἀποκαλοῦσιν; have you learnt
what they do whom men call so and so? Xen.

enclit. Particle, serving to express belief in an assertion,
let me tell you, surely, verily, used to express an inference,
then, consequently, Hom.; and in Trag., to introduce a general sentiment.

of or
for the bow, Aesch.:— ἡ τοξική (sc. τέχνη)
archery, Plat.

of or
from a he-goat: ὁ τραγείη (sc. δορά)
a goat's skin, Theocr.

a dye
from the shrub ὕσγη,
scarlet, Anth.

c. gen. rei,
to come short of, come too late for, Dem.;
to lag behind, Xen.

in dialogue, φαίνεταί σοι ταῦτα;
does this appear so? is not this so? Answ. φαίνεται,
yes, Plat.; [τοῦτο] φῆις εἶναι; Answ. φαίνομαι (sc. λέγειν) Xen.

of or
for the φαλλός:—τὸ φαλλικόν (sc. μέλος)
the phallic song, Ar.

meanness, paltriness, pettiness, badness, of persons and things, Xen., etc.; ἡ φ. τῶν στρατηγῶν their
want of skill, Dem.;
lack of judgment, Xen.

to love, regard with affection, Lat. diligere, Hom., etc.; φ. τινὰ φιλότητα
to feel affection for him, Od.;
to love and cherish as one's wife, Hom.:—the epic aor1 mid. in act. sense, Il.

flame, blaze, as of lightning, Eur.;
fiery heat, Aesch.;
feverish heat, Luc.

Pass.
to be borne violently along, be hurried along, Trag.;
to be storm-tost, Ar.;
to be carried away, Thuc.

of or
for a φυλέτης,
tribal, Arist.

of or
from Chalastra on the Thermaic gulf:— τὸ Χαλαστραῖον (sc. νίτρον),
soda, found there, and used for purposes of cleansing, Plat.

of or
for a day-labourer: τὸ χ.,
the proletariate, Arist.

like χρῆμα,
a thing, τί χρέος; = τί χρῆμα;
wherefore? Aesch.; ἐφʼ ὅ τι χρ. ἐμόλετε; Eur.

χρῆμα is often expressed where it might be omitted, δεινὸν χρ. ἐποιεῦντο Hdt.; ἐς ἀφανὲς χρ. ἀποστέλλειν ἀποικίαν to send out a colony without any certain destination, id=Hdt.; τί χρῆμα; like τί;
what? τί χρῆμα δρᾷς; Soph.; τί χρῆμα πάσχω; τί δʼ ἐστὶ χρῆμα; what is
the matter? Aesch., etc.

of or
for a light-armed soldier (ψιλός): τὰ ψιλικά, = οἱ ψιλοί,
the light troops, Luc.
